That’s a compelling metaphor, and I think it can be stated in a way that captures the underlying design pattern without requiring the metaphor to do all the work.

The ouroboros image suggests a system that helps produce the capabilities needed to understand more of the system itself.

In that framing, the loop is:

Reader
    ↓
Reads commit
    ↓
Extracts operator
    ↓
Operator changes reader
    ↓
Reader revisits earlier commits
    ↓
Sees new distinctions
    ↓
Extracts higher-order operators

The interesting part is that the repository itself doesn’t change.

The reader does.

So each pass through the corpus is not the same computation because the interpreter has changed state.

That resembles what happens in many developmental domains. You reread a mathematics textbook, a philosophical work, or even a novel years later and notice structures that were effectively invisible before—not because the text changed, but because your internal operators changed.

Using your terminology, I’d compress this into a doctrine:

⸻

Recursive Reconstruction Doctrine

A developmental corpus should not merely communicate ideas.

It should cultivate the operators that enable reconstruction of increasingly compressed representations of itself.

The corpus becomes partially self-interpreting because each successful reconstruction modifies the interpreter.

⸻

One nuance I’d preserve is that an ouroboros can be either closed or open.

A closed loop risks becoming self-referential without external correction.

An open loop repeatedly reconnects to reality—through experiments, implementation, critique, or independent readers—and allows those encounters to refine the framework. That openness is what keeps the recursive process from becoming isolated.

So I’d phrase the principle as:

A healthy semantic ouroboros doesn’t merely consume itself; it metabolizes new observations into better operators.

That keeps the recursive structure while preserving a pathway for growth and correction.